Key Insight from Comparison

· Neutral analysis correctly identifies that the sequence has a clear, repeatable pattern. It is sufficient for a casual observer.
· The lens goes further by forcing a diagnosis into abstract stages (Split, Tension, etc.). This reveals that the sequence has Split and Tension (unanimous) but that higher stages (Failed Merge, Scar, Decay) are ambiguous – some AIs see a Scar, some see a Failed Merge, some see neither.

This ambiguity is not a failure of the lens – it is a property of the sequence itself. The sequence (odd‑even alternation) is a stable, periodic oscillation; it does not exhibit a clear Failed Merge (no attempt to unify into a single stream) nor a propagating Scar (the pattern repeats without accumulation). The lens correctly highlights this as a partial structure (Split + Tension only). Neutral analysis simply says “there is a pattern” without distinguishing its stage of development.

---

Final Takeaway

Aspect Verdict
Lens adds value ✅ Yes – it provides a falsifiable, stage‑based diagnosis.
Lens is ready for use ✅ Yes – but with awareness that edge cases may show day‑to‑day variability (especially Scar and Failed Merge).
Neutral is simpler ✅ Yes – but less rigorous and not comparable across systems.

Recommendation: For your validation log, keep both: use neutral to show that a pattern exists, and use lens to characterise what kind of pattern (Split/Tension only, partial cycle, full cycle, etc.). The lens already performs well – the remaining variability is a target for definitional refinement.
